{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.7,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U5", "R7", "D5", "J1", "J7"],
      "pins": ["U5.30", "J1.2", "J1.3", "J7.2", "J7.3"],
      "nets": ["NET0005", "+12V"],
      "observed": "On the Power sheet the +12V rail (barrel-jack input) is tied to global net NET0005, which feeds fan-header pins 2/3 of J1 and J7. On the MCU sheet the same global net NET0005 is connected to ESP32-C3 pin 30 (GPIO20/U0RXD) together with a 10 k pull-up (R7) to +3V3 and a TVS diode (D5) to GND. Thus the 12 V supply rail and the 3.3 V GPIO/fan-tach input net are merged.",
      "expected": "The fan-tach / GPIO20 input should sit on its own low-voltage net pulled up to +3V3 and clamped by a TVS, while the fan supply pins 2/3 should be on the +12V net; the two must not share a net. The spec describes the tach inputs as 'TVS-diode-protected inputs' on the 3.3 V logic side, never on 12 V.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet (page 7): '+12V' arrow -> node -> 'NET0005' -> J1/J7 Conn_01x04 pins 2,3"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "MCU sheet (page 3): 'NET0005' on U5 pin 30 (GPIO20/U0RXD) with R7 10K to +3V3 and D5 D_TVS to GND"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Monitors 2 fan tachometers (NUM_FANS = 2) with TVS-diode-protected inputs"}
      ],
      "impact": "12 V is applied directly to a 3.3 V GPIO (GPIO20/U0RXD) and to its 3.3 V pull-up and ground-clamp TVS, which will forward-conduct the TVS, fight the pull-up, and likely damage the MCU or the UART RX; the second fan tach is also shorted to 12 V and non-functional.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    },
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["D6"],
      "pins": ["D6.2"],
      "nets": ["NET0004"],
      "observed": "The WS2812B status LED D6 has its data input pin 2 (DIN) wired to net NET0004, but NET0004 appears nowhere else in the drawing; no ESP32 pin (or any other component) carries NET0004. The LED's VDD and GND are connected, but DIN is left floating.",
      "expected": "The onboard WS2812B status LED's DIN must be driven from an ESP32 GPIO, as the spec states the board 'exposes status over ... a single onboard WS2812B status LED'.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "MCU sheet (page 3): 'D6 WS2812B' pin 2 'DIN' -> 'NET0004' (NET0004 has no other connection on any sheet)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "a single onboard WS2812B status LED (D1)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The status LED cannot be addressed by the firmware; the Wi-Fi status indication via the onboard LED is lost.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U8", "C4"],
      "pins": ["U8.6", "U8.5"],
      "nets": ["BST", "SW"],
      "observed": "On the Power sheet the AP63203WU buck U8 bootstrap pin 6 (BST) is tied through C4 (100 nF) to GND, and there is no capacitor between BST and the switching node pin 5 (SW). The reference design's bootstrap cap is therefore absent/misconnected.",
      "expected": "Per the spec, 'U8 SW(5) drives the buck inductor L1 (3.9 µH) and bootstrap cap C13' — the bootstrap capacitor must connect BST to SW, not to GND, to provide high-side gate drive.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet (page 7): U8 pin 6 'BST' -> C4 100nF -> GND; pin 5 'SW' -> L2 3.9uH with no BST-SW cap"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "U8 SW(5) drives the buck inductor L1 (3.9 µH) and bootstrap cap C13"}
      ],
      "impact": "Without a proper BST-SW bootstrap capacitor the high-side MOSFET cannot be turned on, so the 3.3 V rail may fail to start or regulate, starving the MCU and peripherals.",
      "confidence": 0.4
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing deviates from the intended design in at least two clear ways: the +12V rail is bridged onto the 3.3V GPIO20/fan-tach net (NET0005), and the WS2812B status LED's data line (NET0004) is left unconnected to the MCU; a third, less certain issue is the buck bootstrap capacitor appearing tied to GND instead of between BST and SW."
}

